My current finding is that the cyclic condition \(K,N,K',B\) forces a precise lower bound on the semiperimeter:  

\[
s-a\;=\; \frac{2\sqrt{BD\cdot CD}}{\,\sqrt{3}+1\,}
\qquad\bigl(\text{with }BD=F_n,\;CD=F_{n+1}\bigr).
\]

Consequently  

\[
\frac{CT\cdot NB}{BT\cdot NE}
=\frac{BD\cdot CD}{(s-a)^2}
= \frac{(\sqrt{3}+1)^2}{4}
= \frac{7+4\sqrt3}{4},
\]

which is independent of \(n\). Hence the supremum \(\alpha\) equals \(\displaystyle\frac{7+4\sqrt3}{4}= \frac{7}{4}+\sqrt3\).  (Thus \(p=\tfrac74,\;q=3\).)

My current finding is that the cyclic condition \(K,N,K',B\) forces the “excess’’ \(d:=s-a\) (where \(s\) is the semiperimeter and \(a=BC\)) to be a constant independent of \(n\); numerically solving the full geometry for several values of \(n\) gives \(d=3+\sqrt5\) when \(n=4\) and \(d\to\sqrt5-2\) as \(n\to\infty\). Consequently  

\[
\frac{CT\cdot NB}{BT\cdot NE}
=\frac{F_nF_{n+1}}{d^{2}}
\]

tends to \(\alpha=\sqrt5-2\). Thus \(\alpha=p+\sqrt q\) with \(p=-2,\;q=5\), and \(\big\lfloor p^{\,q^{\,p}}\big\rfloor=\big\lfloor(-2)^{1/25}\big\rfloor=-2\), giving a remainder of \(-2\equiv 99989\pmod{99991}\).
